"# Two-tone bell

Print sheet · 2026-10-21 · [Open this lamp in openlamp](https://jahoefne.github.io/openlamp/#p=TEST)

- **Lamp:** Pendant on a cord (600 mm drop, knotted in the canopy); USB LED puck (Bambu Lab LED Lamp Kit-001, Ø59 × 8 mm); shade on a bayonet joint
- **Printer:** Bambu Lab A1, bed 256 × 256 × 256 mm, 0.4 mm nozzle, multi-colour (AMS lite)
- **Export:** 4 STL files, one per part, at normal quality

## Parts

| Part | Qty | Colour | PLA | Triangles | Print orientation | Joints |
| :-- | --: | :-- | --: | --: | :-- | :-- |
| Hub | 1 | black \`#1f1f23\` | — | — | Collar ring on the bed, mount face up | bayonet for the Shade; bayonet for the Inner shade |
| Shade | 1 | black \`#1f1f23\` | — | — | Open end on the bed, collar up | bayonet into the Hub |
| Inner shade | 1 | white \`#ffffff\` | — | — | Open end on the bed, collar up | bayonet into the Hub |
| Canopy | 1 | black \`#1f1f23\` | — | — | Ceiling face on the bed | — |
| **Total** | **4** |  | — |  |  |  |

PLA: estimated weight of all copies; "—" until the part is built in openlamp. Triangles: of one STL.

## Also needed

- 1 × USB LED puck: Bambu Lab LED Lamp Kit-001 (Ø59 × 8 mm) or any puck of that size, with its USB cable
- 1 × USB power adapter (5 V)
- Cord Ø4 mm, at least 800 mm (the 600 mm drop and the knots) plus the run from the canopy to the power: the puck's USB cable or a USB extension
- 2 × ceiling screws up to Ø4 mm with heads up to Ø9 mm, and wall plugs, for the canopy's slots (60 mm apart)

## Assembly

1. Feed the cord down through the hub's cord boss, pull it out through the light opening, tie a knot and pull it back up into the knot cavity.
2. Press the LED puck into the hub from the light face until it stops; the crush ribs hold it.
3. Feed the other end of the cord up through the canopy's cord hole and tie a knot 600 mm above the hub, so it sits in the canopy's recess.
4. Screw the canopy to the ceiling through its two slots (60 mm apart) with the screws and wall plugs; run the cable on to the power supply.
5. Fit the shade to the hub: push its lugs into the slots of the hub's ring and turn it clockwise (seen from the shade's open end) until it stops.

## Printing

- 0.4 mm nozzle, 0.2 mm layers (0.12 mm for finer ribs). Every part prints as exported, without supports.
- Shades: a 0.8 mm wall (2 perimeters), which diffuses the light best, and no infill. White or "Jade White" PLA spreads the light most evenly.
- No brim (brim marks show under light): a smooth PEI plate and a slow first layer hold the thin wall.
- Exported at normal quality: draft is for quick test prints, normal is plenty for printing, fine gives smoother ribs and much larger files.

### Hub

File: \`openlamp-two-tone-bell-hub.stl\`

- Print as exported: collar ring on the bed, mount face up. No supports needed.
- Feed the cord down through the boss, pull it out through the light opening, tie a knot and pull it back up into the knot cavity; then press the puck in from below until it stops (crush ribs hold it).

### Shade

File: \`openlamp-two-tone-bell-shade.stl\`

- Print as exported: open end on the bed, collar up. No supports needed.
- Two 0.4 mm perimeters (0.8 mm wall) diffuse the light best; no infill needed.
- Assembly: push the shade's lugs up into the hub's slots and turn the shade clockwise (seen from below) until it stops.
- Hang the inner shade first, then this (outer) shade.

### Inner shade

File: \`openlamp-two-tone-bell-innerShade.stl\`

- Print as exported: open end on the bed, collar up. No supports needed.
- Two 0.4 mm perimeters (0.8 mm wall) diffuse the light best; no infill needed.
- Assembly: push the shade's lugs up into the hub's slots and turn the shade clockwise (seen from below) until it stops.
- Hang this inner shade from the inner collar ring first, then the outer shade around it.

### Canopy

File: \`openlamp-two-tone-bell-canopy.stl\`

- Print as exported: ceiling face on the bed. No supports needed.
- Feed the cord up through the canopy and tie a knot that sits in the recess; then screw the canopy to the ceiling through its two slots.

## Warnings

- ℹ innerShade does not fit on the same plate as the other parts; print it separately.
- ℹ hub does not fit on the same plate as the other parts; print it separately.
- ℹ canopy does not fit on the same plate as the other parts; print it separately.

---

Made with openlamp, https://jahoefne.github.io/openlamp/
"
`;

exports[`the print sheet of wall-lamp.lamp.json > matches its snapshot 1`] = `
"# Wall lamp

Print sheet · 2026-10-21 · [Open this lamp in openlamp](https://jahoefne.github.io/openlamp/#p=TEST)

- **Lamp:** Wall arm (angled, 200 mm); USB LED puck (Bambu Lab LED Lamp Kit-001, Ø59 × 8 mm); shade on a bayonet joint
- **Printer:** Bambu Lab A1, bed 256 × 256 × 256 mm, 0.4 mm nozzle, multi-colour (AMS lite)
- **Export:** 4 STL files, one per part, at normal quality

## Parts

| Part | Qty | Colour | PLA | Triangles | Print orientation | Joints |
| :-- | --: | :-- | --: | --: | :-- | :-- |
| Hub | 1 | warm light grey \`#d9d4cb\` | — | — | Collar ring on the bed, mount face up | bayonet for the Shade; thread Ø16 × 2.5 into the Wall arm |
| Shade | 1 | cream \`#f2e8d8\` | — | — | Open end on the bed, collar up | bayonet into the Hub |
| Wall plate | 1 | warm light grey \`#d9d4cb\` | — | — | Back face on the bed | 4 × M3 (heat-set inserts) for the Wall arm |
| Wall arm | 1 | warm light grey \`#d9d4cb\` | — | — | Standing on its flange, the end boss's thread lying horizontal | thread Ø16 × 2.5 for the Hub; 4 × M3 into the Wall plate |
| **Total** | **4** |  | — |  |  |  |

PLA: estimated weight of all copies; "—" until the part is built in openlamp. Triangles: of one STL.

## Also needed

- 1 × USB LED puck: Bambu Lab LED Lamp Kit-001 (Ø59 × 8 mm) or any puck of that size, with its USB cable
- 1 × USB power adapter (5 V)
- 2 × wall screws up to Ø4 mm with heads up to Ø8.5 mm, and wall anchors, 60 mm apart on a vertical line
- Cable Ø6 mm, at least 500 mm through the arm plus the run to the power: the puck's USB cable or a USB extension
- 4 × M3 × 10 screws (Wall arm to Wall plate)
- 4 × M3 heat-set inserts (and a soldering iron to set them)

## Assembly

1. Mark two holes 60 mm apart on a vertical line, drill them and set the wall anchors; drive the screws in, leaving their heads about 3 mm proud.
2. Press four M3 heat-set inserts into the pockets on the wall plate's front face, flush.
3. Feed the cable in through the arm's flange and out of the threaded bore at its end.
4. Press the LED puck into the hub from the light face until it stops (the crush ribs hold it); its cable runs through the bore in the hub's mount face.
5. Screw the hub onto the arm, clockwise seen from below.
6. Screw the arm's flange to the plate with four M3 × 10 screws, the cable lying in the plate's slot.
7. Hang the plate on the two screws (heads through the keyholes, then slide it down); the cable leaves through the slot at its bottom edge.
8. Fit the shade to the hub: push its lugs into the slots of the hub's ring and turn it clockwise (seen from the shade's open end) until it stops.

## Printing

- 0.4 mm nozzle, 0.2 mm layers (0.12 mm for finer ribs). Every part prints as exported, without supports.
- Shades: a 0.8 mm wall (2 perimeters), which diffuses the light best, and no infill. White or "Jade White" PLA spreads the light most evenly.
- No brim on the shades (brim marks show under light): a smooth PEI plate and a slow first layer hold the thin wall. The wall arm is the exception: give the tall part a brim.
- Exported at normal quality: draft is for quick test prints, normal is plenty for printing, fine gives smoother ribs and much larger files.

### Hub

File: \`openlamp-wall-lamp-hub.stl\`

- Print as exported: collar ring on the bed, mount face up. No supports needed.
- Press the puck in from below until it stops (crush ribs hold it); its cable runs up through the bore in the mount face.

### Shade

File: \`openlamp-wall-lamp-shade.stl\`

- Print as exported: open end on the bed, collar up. No supports needed.
- Two 0.4 mm perimeters (0.8 mm wall) diffuse the light best; no infill needed.
- Assembly: push the shade's lugs up into the hub's slots and turn the shade clockwise (seen from below) until it stops.

### Wall plate

File: \`openlamp-wall-lamp-wallPlate.stl\`

- Print as exported: back face on the bed. No supports needed.
- Hang the plate on two screws through its keyholes (heads in the channels on the back, the plate slid down onto them); the cable leaves through the slot at its bottom edge.
- Press four M3 heat-set inserts into the pockets on the front face, flush.

### Wall arm

File: \`openlamp-wall-lamp-wallArm.stl\`

- Print as exported: standing on its flange, the end boss's thread lying horizontal. No supports needed; a brim keeps the tall part in place.
- Feed the cable in through the flange and out of the threaded bore, screw the hub onto the arm (clockwise seen from below), then screw the flange to the plate with four M3 × 10 screws.

## Warnings

None.

---

Made with openlamp, https://jahoefne.github.io/openlamp/
"
`;

